INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, disclosure_label, disclosure_text, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261127-op-nvidia-board-huang', 'nvidia-board-folded-six-days-jensen-huang-reinstatement-not-acceptable', 'newsroom', 'opinion', 'columns', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Nvidia''s Board Folded in Six Days. That Is Not Acceptable.', 'Nvidia''s board said it would not bring Jensen Huang back, then reinstated him six days later, before its own investigation was finished. That is not how the board of America''s most important AI chip company should act.', 'On November 17, Nvidia''s board chair, Harvey Jones, said the board "does not intend to reinstate Jensen as CEO." Six days later, the same board voted unanimously to put Jensen Huang back in charge.

Boards change course all the time. But the board of the world''s most important AI chip company reversing itself this fast, on a question this serious, with this little explanation, is something else. It is simply not acceptable.

Start with how we got here. On October 29, The New York Times reported that U.S. intelligence officials believed an executive at Megaspeed, a Singapore-based Nvidia customer, was a Chinese intelligence asset who had developed a close relationship with Mr. Huang. China''s Foreign Ministry called the reports "baseless and purely slanderous smears." Nvidia said his interactions with her were "not unusual or unexpected." On November 8, Mr. Huang admitted an affair, and the board opened an investigation into whether he was the target of a foreign intelligence effort. On November 12, he resigned.

Let me be clear about what is not proven. Nobody has shown that Jensen Huang knowingly helped China or passed it anything. A Justice Department official says the department has no evidence of wrongdoing by him or by Nvidia, and Megaspeed denies wrongdoing too. He is entitled to that presumption, and this column does not ask anyone to convict him.

My complaint is with the board.

First, it decided before it knew. The board''s own committee of independent directors is still investigating whether the CEO was targeted by a foreign intelligence service. The November 23 announcement says that investigation "will proceed to conclusion." That is backwards. You finish the investigation, then decide who leads. When the verdict comes before the evidence, the evidence stops mattering.

Second, it asks us to trust what it will not show us. The board says it is "confident that Mr. Huang''s reinstatement poses no national security risk" because of "input from the senior levels of the United States government." What input, and from whom? President Trump publicly urged the board to rehire Mr. Huang the day before the vote, and afterward told reporters, "Basically, I was represented at the board." Mr. Huang has told employees that the claim Trump got him reinstated is "completely untrue." Only the board can settle which is true. It has not. Senator Elizabeth Warren says the administration "needs to explain to Congress what ''input'' was given." So does the board.

Third, it looks like the market made the decision. While Mr. Huang was out, stocks fell for seven straight sessions and the S&P 500 slipped into correction territory. When he came back, Nvidia''s shares jumped about 30 percent in a single day. President Trump himself said the market declines "probably" affected his thinking. A board''s duty is to the long-term health of the company, not to one bad week of trading. If governance can be reversed by a selloff, it is not governance at all.

Fourth, the stakes are national. Nvidia''s chips sit at the center of U.S. export controls and the race for artificial intelligence. That is exactly why its leader must be beyond doubt. The Wall Street Journal reported that Mr. Huang disclosed additional affairs to the board before the vote, and the board went ahead anyway. Mr. Huang has admitted he violated the company''s code of conduct. Every engineer and salesperson at Nvidia is held to that code. A rule that bends for the man at the top teaches everyone below him that rules are for other people.

As someone who implements AI systems for a living, I know how much this industry depends on Nvidia, and I do not doubt Mr. Huang''s brilliance. But brilliance is not a security clearance, and founders are not above governance. The question was never whether he is talented. It was whether his board would do its job when it mattered most. It did not.

The board did announce a new National Security and Export Control Committee of independent directors and a search for a Chief Compliance Officer. Both are welcome, but they arrive after the decision they should have informed.

Here is what a responsible board would have done. Keep the interim leadership in place. Let the independent investigation finish. Publish its findings, with only the redactions national security truly requires. Then decide. That process might well have brought Mr. Huang back, and if the facts had cleared him, I would have said so in these pages.

Some of the damage can still be repaired. Mr. Huang is due to answer questions privately from House committees on December 1 and to testify publicly before the Senate Banking Committee on December 3. He should answer every question fully. When the board''s investigation concludes, Nvidia should publish its findings, not bury them in a footnote.

Trust in a company that powers America''s AI future cannot rest on "trust us." Until Nvidia''s board shows its work, its decision to bring Jensen Huang back is simply not acceptable.

*This article represents the opinion of Shaheen Arif, Editor of Boston Daily.*', 'Shaheen Arif | Editor, Boston Daily', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Jensen_Huang_at_Computex_Taipei_20160531c.jpg', 'Jensen Huang speaks at Computex in Taipei in 2016 (file photo). Photo: NVIDIA Taiwan / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0', 'licensed', NULL, NULL, 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-27-nvidia-huang-column.sql', 7, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-27T22:40:00.000Z', '2026-11-27T22:40:00.000Z');

